{
  "description": "Controlled vocabulary of pre-1832 historic counties of England, Wales, and Scotland. Used by the HCA knowledge graph ingestor to infer country from county when creating place hierarchy edges. The segmenter extracts county from explicit textual cues; the ingestor uses this file to look up the country.",
  "source": "Historic Counties Standard (pre-1832 boundaries). England: 39 counties. Wales: 12 counties (from Laws in Wales Acts, 1535 and 1543). Monmouthshire: 1 (sui generis — ambiguous English/Welsh status). Scotland: 34 counties (pre-1889; Ross-shire and Cromartyshire listed separately, as they were distinct counties in the early modern period before being merged into Ross and Cromarty).",
  "last_updated": "2026-04-23",
  "version": "1.0",
  "usage": "Match the county extracted by the segmenter's place_components.county field against the normalised_name (case-insensitive). If matched, the country field provides the deterministic country value for the ingestor to use when creating within edges. The variants array lists spelling forms observed in HCA deposition front matter — this will grow as more volumes are processed. If a county string does not match any entry (including variants), flag for human review rather than guessing.",
  "notes": "WORK IN PROGRESS. Spelling variants are drawn from HCA 13/71 only. As additional volumes are processed, new variants should be added. The list covers England, Wales, and Scotland. Irish counties are out of scope for now (Irish deponents may appear but are rare in the HCA 13 series). Channel Islands (Jersey, Guernsey) are not counties but may appear in HCA depositions — they are listed separately at the end.",
